                <pb facs="0559" n="553" sameAs="frus:frus1939v04/pg553"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890G.42/38: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister Resident in Iraq</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Knabenshue</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Baghdad</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-11-10T19:00:00+03:00">November 10, 1939—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 10—9:45 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>72. Referring to the Department’s telegram number 39, November 9, 1 p.m. The
                    present status of proposed education law was reported <pb facs="0560" n="554"
                        xml:id="pg554"/> in my despatch No. 1427 dated November 2, sent by pouch on
                    that date to the following effect: I sent a note to the Ministry of Foreign
                    Affairs on October 16 in the sense suggested in the Department’s instruction
                    September 14. In the meantime the British Ambassador acting upon instructions
                    from London is giving full support to my representations. I am, therefore,
                    hopeful of a reasonably satisfactory solution.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Knabenshue</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
